Your intuition is pointing at a real issue, but I think the conclusion “therefore FOM should be added to SRMC in the clearing stack” is not quite right.

The key distinction is between:

1. SRMC / economic marginal-cost stack


2. The generator’s actual offer/bid stack


3. The price required for the plant to recover all its costs over time



Those are not the same thing.

My verdict on the claim in the screenshot

The screenshot is mostly correct that fixed O&M should not simply be added to SRMC for marginal dispatch.

However, I think it makes another important mistake: it says FOM should instead be put into the commit/run threshold. If this is genuinely annual fixed O&M, that is also questionable.

AEMO defines fixed O&M as a recurring annual cost that occurs regardless of the variation in generator output, while VOM varies with output. The AER explicitly uses that distinction in its generator-cost methodology. 

So the clean economic structure is closer to:

\text{SRMC} =
\text{Fuel}
+
\text{VOM}
+
\text{emissions/opportunity costs}

not:

\text{SRMC} =
\text{Fuel}
+
\text{VOM}
+
\text{FOM allocation}

An Australian regulatory modelling document explicitly defines SRMC as fuel + variable O&M + carbon where applicable, while treating fixed O&M as part of longer-run cost. 

But there is a subtlety here that is very relevant to what you are building.


---

Your argument: “Why would a coal generator generate at $65 if it needs $68 including FOM?”

Suppose Bayswater has:

Fuel + VOM = 64.7

and annual FOM allocated mechanically across 8,760 hours gives:

FOM = 3.2/MWh

So an “all-in operating cost” allocation would be:

67.9/MWh

Suppose spot price is:

66/MWh

Your intuition is:

> Why would Bayswater willingly generate at $66? It isn't recovering the $67.9/MWh including fixed OPEX.



The answer is that once the fixed cost exists, generating at $66 is financially better than not generating, assuming the unit is available/online and there are no other incremental costs.

Generate:

66-64.7=+1.3/MWh

That $1.30 is contribution toward fixed costs.

Don't generate:

0

You still have the annual FOM obligation.

So your choices are effectively:

\text{Generate: FOM shortfall reduced by }1.3

versus

\text{Don't generate: no contribution toward FOM}

That is why a generator can rationally operate at a price below average total cost but above marginal cost.

This is standard marginal economics.

And for coal it can go even further: because shutdown and restart are costly, coal generators sometimes offer capacity at very low or even negative prices to remain dispatched. The AER explicitly notes that coal plants have high startup costs and that paying to continue generating for a limited period can be cheaper than shutting down and restarting. 

So the proposition:

> “A rational coal generator would never willingly generate below SRMC + allocated FOM”



is not correct.

It absolutely might.


---

But here is where I think your concern is valid

You are talking about a clearing stack.

And this creates an important distinction the screenshot somewhat glosses over:

The NEM does not actually clear an “SRMC stack”.

It clears an offer stack.

AEMO orders generators' offers from cheaper to more expensive and dispatches accordingly. 

The AER describes the price-setting mechanism similarly: participants offer capacity in price bands, and generally the highest-priced offer required to meet demand determines the dispatch price. 

Therefore:

\boxed{\text{Actual offer price} \neq \text{necessarily SRMC}}

A generator can offer:

Bid > SRMC

or:

Bid < SRMC

depending on startup/shutdown economics, minimum generation, hedge positions, fuel constraints, opportunity cost, market power and commercial strategy.

The AER specifically documents coal generators offering capacity at negative prices to remain dispatched because avoiding shutdown/restart can be economically preferable. 

So there are really two separate questions.


---

1. Should FOM be included in SRMC?

No.

If Bayswater has:

Fuel = $56.7/MWh

VOM = $8/MWh


then:

\boxed{SRMC=64.7/MWh}

Calling:

64.7+3.2=67.9

“SRMC” would be economically incorrect if the $3.20 is simply annual fixed O&M divided by expected MWh or hours.

The AER/AEMO treatment explicitly distinguishes FOM from costs varying with output. 


---

2. Should your modelled clearing stack necessarily use pure SRMC?

This is where the answer is: not necessarily.

And I think this is probably the actual issue you're getting at.

If your model is intended to simulate:

> “What would the NEM price be based on generators' commercial willingness to offer?”



then blindly putting every coal generator into the stack at:

Fuel+VOM

may indeed produce unrealistic prices.

But the solution is not necessarily:

Bid=SRMC+FOM

The better structure is:

\boxed{BidPrice=f(SRMC,\ commitment,\ contracts,\ startup,\ opportunity\ cost,\ strategy,\ market\ conditions)}

In other words, you need to distinguish:

Cost stack

Generator       Economic SRMC
Solar              ~0
Wind               ~0
Brown coal          30
Black coal          65
CCGT                100
OCGT                180

from:

Offer stack

Generator          Offer price
Solar             -1,000 / 0 / etc.
Coal block 1      -1,000
Coal block 2        20
Coal block 3        65
Coal block 4        90
Gas                150
...

The second one is much closer to how the actual NEM works.

Participants can offer capacity across multiple price bands rather than submitting one single “true cost.” 


---

There is also a major misconception in “if it bids $64.7, it clears at $64.7”

This is probably the most important point.

A generator's offer price is not normally what it gets paid.

Suppose:

Bayswater offer           $64.70
Eraring offer             $70
Vales Point offer         $75
Gas generator             $110   <- marginal generator

Demand requires the gas generator.

The market price might therefore be:

110/MWh

Bayswater doesn't receive $64.70 just because it offered at $64.70.

It receives the regional spot price, subject to the normal NEM settlement mechanics.

The AER explicitly notes that dispatched generators receive the market price rather than their individual offer price. 

So Bayswater earns:

110-64.7=45.3/MWh

of gross contribution.

That contribution is how it can recover:

fixed O&M,

capital costs,

financing costs,

prior losses,

and earn profit.


Therefore a generator does not need to add FOM to every MWh bid in order to recover FOM.

That is fundamental to uniform-price electricity markets.


---

Where does FOM actually get recovered?

Through inframarginal rents across time.

Imagine Bayswater's SRMC is $64.70.

Over five intervals:

Price	Dispatch decision	Contribution above SRMC

$50	potentially don't produce marginal MW	$0
$66	produce	+$1.30
$80	produce	+$15.30
$120	produce	+$55.30
$300	produce	+$235.30


It doesn't need:

P\ge67.9

every single interval.

It needs cumulative revenues over the relevant period to satisfy something like:

\sum (P_t-SRMC_t)Q_t
\ge
FOM + other\ fixed\ costs

over time.

That is a revenue adequacy / profitability condition, not a five-minute marginal dispatch condition.

The AER similarly discusses generators recovering longer-run costs through a mixture of spot-market and contract revenue rather than through a simple fixed-cost adder in every dispatch offer. 


---

Where I disagree with the screenshot

This sentence is problematic:

> “FOM lifts the threshold for being online, not the threshold for the next MWh once online.”



The second half is basically right.

The first half is not necessarily right if we're talking about true annual FOM.

There are actually three decisions:

A. Dispatch another MWh

Relevant costs:

Fuel+VOM+incremental\ wear+emissions/opportunity

This is the SRMC decision.


---

B. Start / stay online

Relevant costs include things such as:

Startup\ cost

No-load\ cost

Minimum\ stable\ generation

Shutdown/restart\ consequences

and expected prices over the next several hours.

NREL's work on electricity-market pricing explicitly distinguishes marginal energy costs from online commitment costs in $/hour and startup costs in $/start. 

So a realistic commitment test looks more like:

Expected\ Revenue
\ge
Fuel+VOM+Startup+NoLoad+other\ avoidable\ operating\ costs

across the commitment horizon.


---

C. Keep Bayswater operating as a business / don't mothball or retire it

Now FOM matters directly.

Expected\ annual\ gross\ margin
\ge
FOM + other\ avoidable\ fixed\ costs

If not, eventually:

Retire/mothball

The AER's cost framework treats fixed-cost savings as arising when generators retire earlier or commence operation later, distinctly from avoided variable costs such as fuel, VOM and startup costs. 

That is the economically cleaner place for annual FOM.


---

So I would change the model architecture

Rather than:

srmc = fuel + vom + fom_per_mwh

or even blindly:

dispatch_srmc = fuel + vom

commit_threshold = fuel + vom + fom_per_hour / capacity

I would use:

marginal_cost = (
    fuel
    + vom
    + emissions
    + other_incremental_cost
)

Then separately:

commitment_cost = (
    startup_cost
    + no_load_cost
    + other_avoidable_online_costs
)

And:

annual_fixed_cost = fixed_om_per_mw_year * capacity

Then the model has three layers:

DECISION                       COST CONCEPT

5-min dispatch    Produce one more MWh?       → SRMC

Commitment        Start/stay online?           → SRMC + startup/no-load
                                               evaluated multi-period

Plant viability   Keep plant open?             → Gross margin vs FOM
                                               and other fixed costs

And then separately:

MARKET PRICE MODELLING

Actual/estimated offer curves
        ↓
Clearing stack
        ↓
Dispatch / marginal offer
        ↓
Spot price


---

The most important conclusion for your clearing-stack model

I would not put FOM into srmc.

But I also would not necessarily use pure SRMC as the generator's offer price.

Those are two different statements.

Your model should probably have:

\boxed{\text{Economic SRMC}}

and separately:

\boxed{\text{Modelled Offer Price}}

For example:

generator.srmc_aud_mwh = 64.7

while:

generator.offer_curve = [
    (-1000, 300),   # must-run/min stable load
    (60,    150),
    (68,    150),
    (85,    150),
    (150,   100),
]

Those offer bands can implicitly reflect commercial cost recovery, commitment economics, hedging and bidding strategy without corrupting the definition of SRMC.

That would also solve your legitimate concern that an SRMC-only merit stack may systematically under-predict coal price-setting behaviour.

Bottom line

Your statement:

> “FOM should be included in SRMC because otherwise the coal generator is willing to clear at a price that doesn't recover fixed OPEX.”



is not correct economically.

But the deeper point you're making:

> “A pure fuel+VOM SRMC stack may not represent the prices at which real coal generators actually offer into the NEM.”



is correct and important.

The fix is not SRMC += FOM. The fix is to separate SRMC, commitment economics, annual fixed-cost recovery, and the actual/modelled bid curve.

And I would specifically scrutinise the existing code that does:

run_threshold = SRMC + FOM + no_load + ...

because no_load and FOM should not automatically be treated as the same type of commitment cost. If that FOM is AEMO-style annual fixed O&M, charging it only for committed_hours effectively turns a fixed annual cost into an avoidable hourly cost, which is conceptually inconsistent with AEMO's own definition of FOM. 

That may be the actual modelling bug here—not whether FOM belongs inside the dispatch SRMC.